<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>OtdInterop.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Microsoft Testing Platform, not VSTest (#756). xunit v3 test projects are executables that
         host their own runner, and OutputType=Exe is what xunit v3 requires once Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk
         is no longer there to set it. Which runner `dotnet test` uses is named once for the repo, in
         global.json, rather than per project:

             "test": { "runner": "Microsoft.Testing.Platform" }

         So xunit.runner.visualstudio, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk and coverlet.collector are gone: the
         first two are the VSTest path, and coverlet.collector is a VSTest data collector that nothing
         collected — no workflow ran with coverage and there is no .runsettings in the tree.

         The global.json route rather than the per-project TestingPlatformDotnetTestSupport property,
         because the two differ in a way that matters (#939). Under the property, `dotnet test` accepts
         a VSTest option such as a filter, warns MTP0001 that it is being ignored, runs the WHOLE suite
         and exits 0 — so narrowing to one test gives a green result for a run nobody asked for, and a
         script requesting coverage or a logger gets the same false success. Under the runner named
         here the same option is refused outright: "Unknown option", exit 5, and no tests run.

         Filtering then uses the platform's own options (filter-class, filter-method, filter-namespace)
         through `dotnet test`. docs/dev/BUILDING.md has the commands and what IDE discovery needs.

         CI is unaffected: it runs `dotnet test <csproj>` in its usual no-build form with no filter, a
         failing test exits 2, and a bad option exits 5 (all checked, not assumed). -->

    <!-- xunit v3, the same line as the other two suites (#756). -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="3.2.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

<ItemGroup>
    <!--
      OtdInterop and nothing else. No OpenTabletArtist, no Avalonia: this suite exists to prove the
      library stands on its own, and a reference here would let that quietly stop being true. The check
      that enforces it is BoundaryDependencyTests, which reads this assembly's actual references rather
      than trusting this file.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\OtdInterop\OtdInterop.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
